        "code": {
          "language": "markdown",
          "showLineNumbers": false,
          "showMiniMap": false
        },
        "content": "Derived proxy, **not** flow data \u2014 read the caveats before drawing a conclusion from it. No metric in this cluster carries a peer/destination label: `kubevirt_vmi_network_*` and `container_network_*` are plain per-interface byte counters, and there is no Hubble, conntrack exporter or other flow telemetry deployed. So this section infers the in-cluster peer axis from the *counterpart pods' own* counters, grouped by the namespace those pods live in.\n\nThe peer set is exactly the in-cluster peers the sandbox NetworkPolicies permit **and** whose traffic is near-dedicated to the sandbox:\n\n- `ai` / `mcp-kubernetes-sandbox-*` \u2014 `apps/subsystems/ai/mcp-kubernetes-sandbox/network-policy.yaml` restricts its egress to kube-dns and `sandbox-talos`/`talos-vm:6443` and nothing else; `sandbox-talos`'s `allow-coder-and-mcp-ingress` admits it on 6443/50000.\n- `sandbox-lifecycle` / `provision-agent-admin-*` \u2014 `allow-egress-sandbox-talos-vm` permits it to `sandbox-talos`/`talos-vm` on 6443/50000. These are short-lived CronJob pods, so cAdvisor only occasionally catches one alive; expect this series to be sparse and to under-count.\n\nDeliberately excluded, and therefore missing from the breakdown:\n\n- **Everything outside the cluster.** Both VMs have wide-open internet egress (`allow-egress-internet`), which is the bulk of their traffic and is not attributable this way at all.\n- **`coder`.** Its namespace *is* permitted into both sandboxes (`allow-coder-ingress` on `sandbox-docker` :22/:6550-6560, `allow-coder-and-mcp-ingress` on `sandbox-talos` :6443/:50000), but coder workspace pods are general-purpose dev environments whose counters are dominated by unrelated traffic \u2014 including them would produce a number that moves for reasons having nothing to do with the sandbox.\n- **`kube-system` (kube-dns) and the ingress-nginx LB VIPs `192.168.8.192/28`.** Permitted egress targets, but both serve the whole cluster, so their counters say nothing about the sandbox.\n- **The `netpol-falsifiability-probe` pods.** They live *inside* `sandbox-docker`/`sandbox-talos`, so grouping them by namespace attributes the sandbox to itself rather than to a peer.\n\nFinally, a peer pod's counter is its *whole* pod traffic, not just its sandbox-facing share \u2014 `mcp-kubernetes-sandbox` also answers its own MCP clients. Treat these as an upper bound on sandbox<->peer traffic. **The by-VM panels above are the authoritative totals.**",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "pluginVersion": "11.2.0",
      "transparent": true,
      "type": "text"
    },
